  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nToday - Saturday: Heavy Rain Trending South\n\nLooking at current observations and satellite analysis, there is an \nMCV that is situated over western Iowa with a boundary positioned \nover northern Iowa. This boundary will gradually shift northward \nthrough the afternoon and evening. The MCV will move eastward \nproviding central and eastern Iowa with a chance for severe storms. \nAs for the boundary across northern Iowa, this will provide the area \nwith scattered showers and some rumbles of thunder during the later \nafternoon and evening hours. The majority of the instability stays \nin Iowa, so northeast Iowa has the best chance (20 to 40%) for a \nthunderstorm developing this afternoon and evening. The trend for \nany heavy rain has continued to shift further south as the axis of \nheavy rain is now more along I-80. While the ERO may have a slight \nrisk for excessive rainfall this evening and early overnight, the \nchances for this happening continue to dwindle given that the better \nforcing will remain out of our forecast area. The MCV will move \neastward across central Iowa and as the LLJ increases this evening, \nwill provide widespread heavy rainfall for eastern Iowa and western \nIllinois. A few days ago there was higher potential for this band of \nheavy rain to impact northeast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin, however \nsimilar to each of the MCS's that have went south of the forecast \narea, this too has trended further south. \n\nSome leftover showers are possible (10 to 20%) for the morning and \nearly afternoon, but the better chances for rain on Saturday arrive \nwith a cold front during the late afternoon/early evening. \nInstability increases as 1000 to 1500 J/kg of MUCAPE build into the \nregion so showers and isolated storms will be possible during this \ntimeframe. There is a little uncertainty on the exact coverage of \nstorms along this front, but for right now the coverage looks to be \nmore isolated (20 to 40%) for showers and storms. Given the \ninstability and weaker shear, if any stronger storm develops, there \nmay be some strong outflow winds from these storms. \n\nSunday - Midweek: Dry Early, More Storm Chances by Tuesday\n\nZonal flow becomes the dominant flow pattern through midweek. A \nsurface high passes through the Upper Midwest on Sunday and Monday \ngiving way to mostly dry conditions and high temperatures in the mid \n70s to low 80s. Heading into Tuesday, a shortwave trough moves \nthrough the region. Moisture tries to get pulled northward as PWATs \nget into the  1.25 to 1.50\" range. Enough 0-6km shear (45 to 60kts) \nwill be in place where the forcing is the best that severe storms \ncould be possible, however this is highly uncertain right now due to \ndifferences amongst models in where the best forcing will be, how \nthe cold front from the night before impacts where storms initiate \non Tuesday, and how much recovery can take place. AI and ML guidance \nalso highlight this day with some low probabilities for severe \nweather to occur. Details regarding the storm potential will be \nrefined as we get closer, but this is something to monitor.  After \nthe trough exits the region on Thursday, ensemble and deterministic \ndata support a switch back to northwest flow, which could lead to \nslightly cooler temperatures for the latter half of next week.",
